@prefix dho:  <https://guoxiangng.github.io/dhamma-ontology/ns#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

# Batch 9 (2026-11-16): SN 46.9, 46.10, 46.39, 46.42, 46.55. Generated by a script: every dho:wording
# and dho:image is sliced from the source files, never typed. Read Pali first (D19).

dho:AppearanceOfRealizedOne a dho:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "the appearance of a Realized One"@en , "tathāgatassa pātubhāva"@pi ;
    skos:altLabel "the appearance of a Buddha"@en ;
    skos:note "'A Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha' (tathāgata arahant sammāsambuddha), SN 46.9 and 46.42."@en .

dho:SugataVinaya a dho:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "the Holy One's training"@en , "sugatavinaya"@pi .

dho:SensualLust a dho:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "sensual lust"@en , "kāmarāga"@pi ;
    skos:note "SN 46.55 opens its five with kāmarāga, not the hindrance's usual name kāmacchanda; the other four are the hindrances' names. Sujato renders both words 'sensual desire'. Kept as its own concept, editor-linked to sensual desire, because the text uses a different word (D23, as for the fetter uddhacca, D13)."@en ;
    dho:editorialRelated dho:SensualDesire .

dho:SeeingWhatIsGood a dho:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "truly knowing and seeing what is good for oneself, for another, and for both"@en ,
        "attattha parattha ubhayattha yathābhūtaṁ jānāti passati"@pi ;
    skos:altLabel "knowing what is good"@en .

dho:c-u1 a dho:Condition ; dho:condition dho:AppearanceOfRealizedOne ; dho:relation dho:RequiredFor ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SevenAwakeningFactors ; dho:result dho:Arises ;
    dho:wording "don’t arise to be developed and cultivated except when a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha has appeared" ;
    skos:note "Pali: 'these seven awakening factors, developed and cultivated, arise unarisen, not apart from (nāññatra) the appearance of a Realized One'. 'To be developed and cultivated' is Sujato's reading of bhāvitā bahulīkatā. A necessary condition, not a sufficient one (D23)."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.9-1.1 , dho:p-sn46.9-1.4 .

dho:c-u2 a dho:Condition ; dho:condition dho:SugataVinaya ; dho:relation dho:RequiredFor ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SevenAwakeningFactors ; dho:result dho:Arises ;
    dho:wording "don’t arise to be developed and cultivated apart from the Holy One’s training" ;
    skos:note "Same form as SN 46.9 (c-u1), with the Holy One's training (sugatavinaya) in place of the appearance of a Realized One."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.10-1.1 , dho:p-sn46.10-1.4 .

dho:c-u3 a dho:Condition ; dho:condition dho:AppearanceOfRealizedOne ; dho:relation dho:WhenThen ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SevenAwakeningFactors ; dho:result dho:Arises ;
    dho:wording "When a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha appears the seven treasures of the awakening factors appear" ;
    skos:note "SN 46.42 calls the factors 'treasures' (bojjhaṅgaratana) and says they appear with (pātubhāvā) a Realized One's appearance; SN 46.9 (c-u1) says they don't arise apart from it. Two claims, kept apart."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.42-2.1 , dho:p-sn46.42-2.4 .

dho:sim-monarch a dho:Simile ;
    skos:prefLabel "the seven treasures of a Wheel-Turning Monarch"@en ;
    dho:image "Mendicants, when a Wheel-Turning Monarch appears seven treasures appear." ;
    dho:wording "When a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha appears the seven treasures of the awakening factors appear." ;
    dho:illustrates dho:c-u3 ;
    skos:note "The text sets the two side by side without 'just as … in the same way' (seyyathāpi … evameva); treated as a simile because the monarch's treasures are there only to illustrate (D23). The seven treasures are named in 1.3."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.42-1.1 , dho:p-sn46.42-1.3 , dho:p-sn46.42-1.4 , dho:p-sn46.42-2.1 .

dho:c-g1 a dho:Condition ; dho:condition dho:SensualLust ; dho:relation dho:WhenThen ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SeeingWhatIsGood ; dho:result dho:IsAbsent ;
    dho:wording "your heart is overcome and mired in sensual desire and you don’t truly understand the escape from sensual desire that has arisen. At that time you don’t truly know or see what is good for yourself, good for another, or good for both." ;
    skos:note "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in kāmarāga, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-3.1 , dho:p-sn46.55-4.3 .
dho:sim-bowl-dyed a dho:Simile ;
    skos:prefLabel "a bowl of water mixed with dye"@en ;
    dho:image "Suppose there was a bowl of water that was mixed with dye such as red lac, turmeric, indigo, or rose madder." ;
    dho:wording "In the same way, when your heart is overcome and mired in sensual desire … Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." ;
    dho:illustrates dho:c-g1 ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-4.1 , dho:p-sn46.55-4.2 , dho:p-sn46.55-4.3 .

dho:c-g2 a dho:Condition ; dho:condition dho:IllWill ; dho:relation dho:WhenThen ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SeeingWhatIsGood ; dho:result dho:IsAbsent ;
    dho:support dho:ExpandedAbbreviation ;
    dho:wording "yasmiṁ samaye byāpādapariyuṭṭhitena cetasā viharati byāpādaparetena, uppannassa ca byāpādassa nissaraṇaṁ yathābhūtaṁ nappajānāti, attatthampi tasmiṁ samaye yathābhūtaṁ na jānāti na passati" ;
    skos:note "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in byāpāda, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind. In both cited segments the Pali gives 'for oneself' and 'for both' in full and abbreviates 'for another' (paratthampi …pe…), which rests on the conventional expansion (D19)."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-5.1 , dho:p-sn46.55-6.3 .
dho:sim-bowl-boiling a dho:Simile ;
    skos:prefLabel "a bowl of water heated and boiling"@en ;
    dho:image "Suppose there was a bowl of water that was heated by fire, boiling and bubbling." ;
    dho:wording "In the same way, when your heart is overcome and mired in ill will … Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." ;
    dho:illustrates dho:c-g2 ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-6.1 , dho:p-sn46.55-6.2 , dho:p-sn46.55-6.3 .

dho:c-g3 a dho:Condition ; dho:condition dho:DullnessDrowsiness ; dho:relation dho:WhenThen ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SeeingWhatIsGood ; dho:result dho:IsAbsent ;
    dho:support dho:ExpandedAbbreviation ;
    dho:wording "yasmiṁ samaye thinamiddhapariyuṭṭhitena cetasā viharati thinamiddhaparetena, uppannassa ca thinamiddhassa nissaraṇaṁ yathābhūtaṁ nappajānāti, attatthampi tasmiṁ samaye yathābhūtaṁ na jānāti na passati" ;
    skos:note "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in thinamiddha, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind. In both cited segments the Pali gives 'for oneself' and 'for both' in full and abbreviates 'for another' (paratthampi …pe…), which rests on the conventional expansion (D19)."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-7.1 , dho:p-sn46.55-8.3 .
dho:sim-bowl-moss a dho:Simile ;
    skos:prefLabel "a bowl of water covered with moss"@en ;
    dho:image "Suppose there was a bowl of water enveloped with moss and aquatic plants." ;
    dho:wording "In the same way, when your heart is overcome and mired in dullness and drowsiness … Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." ;
    dho:illustrates dho:c-g3 ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-8.1 , dho:p-sn46.55-8.2 , dho:p-sn46.55-8.3 .

dho:c-g4 a dho:Condition ; dho:condition dho:RestlessnessRemorse ; dho:relation dho:WhenThen ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SeeingWhatIsGood ; dho:result dho:IsAbsent ;
    dho:support dho:ExpandedAbbreviation ;
    dho:wording "yasmiṁ samaye uddhaccakukkuccapariyuṭṭhitena cetasā viharati uddhaccakukkuccaparetena, uppannassa ca uddhaccakukkuccassa nissaraṇaṁ yathābhūtaṁ nappajānāti, attatthampi tasmiṁ samaye yathābhūtaṁ na jānāti na passati" ;
    skos:note "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in uddhaccakukkucca, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind. In both cited segments the Pali gives 'for oneself' and 'for both' in full and abbreviates 'for another' (paratthampi …pe…), which rests on the conventional expansion (D19)."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-9.1 , dho:p-sn46.55-10.3 .
dho:sim-bowl-wind a dho:Simile ;
    skos:prefLabel "a bowl of water stirred by the wind"@en ;
    dho:image "Suppose there was a bowl of water stirred by the wind, quivering, swirling, and rippling." ;
    dho:wording "In the same way, when your heart is overcome and mired in restlessness and remorse … Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." ;
    dho:illustrates dho:c-g4 ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-10.1 , dho:p-sn46.55-10.2 , dho:p-sn46.55-10.3 .

dho:c-g5 a dho:Condition ; dho:condition dho:Doubt ; dho:relation dho:WhenThen ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SeeingWhatIsGood ; dho:result dho:IsAbsent ;
    dho:wording "yasmiṁ samaye vicikicchāpariyuṭṭhitena cetasā viharati vicikicchāparetena, uppannāya ca vicikicchāya nissaraṇaṁ yathābhūtaṁ nappajānāti, attatthampi tasmiṁ samaye yathābhūtaṁ na jānāti na passati" ;
    skos:note "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in vicikicchā, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-11.1 , dho:p-sn46.55-12.3 .
dho:sim-bowl-muddy a dho:Simile ;
    skos:prefLabel "a bowl of muddy water in the dark"@en ;
    dho:image "Suppose there was a bowl of water that was cloudy, murky, and muddy, hidden in the darkness." ;
    dho:wording "In the same way, there’s a time when your heart is overcome and mired in doubt and you don’t truly understand the escape from doubt that has arisen. At that time you don’t truly know or see what is good for yourself, good for another, or good for both. Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." ;
    dho:illustrates dho:c-g5 ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-12.1 , dho:p-sn46.55-12.2 , dho:p-sn46.55-12.3 .

